🎬 Program 20-Unit III Mini-Project: Media Player with Polymorphic Controls

📌 Description

The Media Player mini-project demonstrates how polymorphism can be used to manage different types of media through a common interface.

The program contains a base class called "Media" and three derived classes:

- 🎵 "Audio"
- 🎥 "Video"
- 🖼️ "Image"

The base class provides common operations such as:

- ▶️ "play()"
- ⏸️ "pause()"
- ⏹️ "stop()"
- ℹ️ "showDetails()"

Each derived class provides its own implementation of these operations.

A collection of "std::unique_ptr<Media>" objects is used to store different media types. Virtual functions ensure that the correct derived-class implementation is executed at run time.

🎯 OOP Concepts Used

- 🔹 Abstract class
- 🔹 Pure virtual functions
- 🔹 Inheritance
- 🔹 Function overriding
- 🔹 Run-time polymorphism
- 🔹 Base-class pointers
- 🔹 Virtual destructor
- 🔹 "std::unique_ptr"
- 🔹 Polymorphic collection

💡 Key Point

One common "Media" interface can control different media types while each derived class provides its own behavior.
